#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119620 :question/id #uuid "94a17888-dcef-58e0-8147-0dec3745f136" :question/math-academy-id "e-4799" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\;\\Pi \\,:\\, \\mathbf{r}=s \\mathbf{b} + t \\mathbf{c}, \\, t,s \\in \\mathbb{R},$ where  $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1, 4, 5 \\rangle, \\mathbf{c} = \\langle 2, 4, 6 \\rangle$ and  $\\mathbf{u}= \\langle 2, 10, 12 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\Pi$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s+2t &=2 \\\\ 4s+4t &=10 \\\\ 5s+6t &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s+2t &=2 \\\\ -4t &=2 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t = 2 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad t = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s+2t & =2 \\\\ s+2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =2 \\\\ s-1 & =2 \\\\ s & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=3, \\, t=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $\\Pi.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=3\\mathbf{b} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a04b551f-3323-5b91-b01e-941912cc92be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308 :topic/difficulty 0.8258457054646602 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428372 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/differential-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1806->1102. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050467} :encompassing/weight 1.0} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428373 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color